    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's birdlife ranges from desert specialists and mountain birds to forest species and seasonal concentrations of waterbirds. Understanding it requires distinguishing year-round residents from breeding visitors, winter visitors, and birds passing through on migration. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Birds", "Migration", "Wetlands", "Endemism", "Hyrcanian forests", "Desert wildlife"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:28:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:28:16Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Native Birds of Iran: Habitats and Migration | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's resident and migratory birds, from the Iranian ground jay to forest specialists, mountain species, and wetland waterbirds."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meaning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Native does not mean present all year",
        "content": "Iran's birds cannot be understood through a single national species total. A bird may breed in Iran and leave for winter, arrive only during the colder months, or stop briefly while travelling between other countries. Some species contain both resident and migratory populations. These distinctions explain why the same landscape can support markedly different bird communities across the year. [s2]\n\nA further distinction is between native and endemic. Native birds occur naturally in a region; endemic species have a much more restricted geographical distribution. The Ornithological Society of the Middle East, the Caucasus and Central Asia identifies the Iranian ground jay as endemic to Iran and the Hyrcanian tit as near endemic. Neither term should be extended to all the country's familiar birds. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-habitats",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A country of contrasting bird habitats",
        "content": "The regional contrasts are striking. The society's country profile associates the Iranian ground jay and greater hoopoe-lark with central Iran, while the northern Hyrcanian forests support birds such as the black woodpecker and Hyrcanian tit. Highlands provide a different setting, with species including the Caspian snowcock and white-winged snowfinch. Western oak forests and southern coasts add further communities. [s1]\n\nThese examples offer a more useful introduction than treating Iran as uniformly desert. They also explain why a national checklist is not a local identification guide: a species recorded somewhere in Iran may be absent from another region or present there only seasonally. Habitat and time of year are essential parts of interpreting a record. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "wetlands",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Wetlands link Iran to distant breeding grounds",
        "content": "Encyclopaedia Iranica's historical synthesis describes the southern Caspian wetlands as important breeding, wintering, and passage habitat. It also documents wetland systems in Khuzestan, Fars, and Sistan, showing that waterbird habitat extends far beyond the northern coast. Its older counts are historical observations, not a current inventory of abundance or wetland condition. [s2]\n\nMiankaleh provides a concrete example. On the Caspian coast, this biosphere reserve combines wetlands and terrestrial habitats, including sand dunes. UNESCO describes it as a staging area for migrant birds and identifies waterbirds among the features supporting its international conservation importance. Autumn and winter birdwatching are part of the area's tourism, alongside livelihoods such as fishing and animal husbandry. [s3]\n\nThe conservation significance therefore extends beyond birds alone. Miankaleh's account also identifies fish spawning grounds, nursery areas, and migration routes. Protecting a wetland involves maintaining this broader ecological setting rather than preserving a list of bird names in isolation. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "pressures-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading change carefully",
        "content": "The ornithological society identifies drought, dams, habitat loss, and illegal hunting as pressures on resident and migratory birds. Their effects need to be assessed at the habitats and seasons involved; a national checklist cannot establish whether a particular population is increasing or declining. [s1]\n\nHistorical accounts remain valuable for understanding migration and regional distributions, but their old abundance estimates should not be relabelled as present conditions. A useful modern assessment would compare repeated counts with consistent timing, coverage, and species identification. Until such observations are assembled, Iran's birdlife is best introduced through its habitats, seasonal movements, and distinctive species rather than an unsupported national trend. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
